SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt: IMF Reaches Staff-Level Agreement on an Extended Fund Facility Arrangement","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-10-27","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/10/26/pr22363-egypt-imf-reaches-staff-level-agreement-on-an-extended-fund-facility-arrangement","claim":"IMF staff and Egyptian authorities reached a staff-level agreement on a 46-month EFF whose package aimed to safeguard macroeconomic stability, strengthen the social safety net, reduce the state footprint, and improve governance and transparency.","locator":"lines 238-253","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct IMF press release describing the agreed policy package and quoting the mission chief.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period fit.","ruler_attribution":"The agreement concerns policies publicly backed by Sisi's government; it is attributable as an official commitment, not as proof of personal candor or self-dealing.","contrary_evidence":["AFTE later said the government's reform rhetoric did not translate into a real political opening and that implementation remained slow and selective."],"lenses":["7B.8","7B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Approves 46-month US$3 billion Extended Arrangement for Egypt","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/12/16/pr22441-egypt-imf-executive-board-approves-46-month-usd3b-extended-arrangement","claim":"The IMF approved Egypt's EFF and said the program would restore buffers, reduce the state footprint, strengthen governance and transparency, and support social safety nets and private-sector-led growth.","locator":"lines 256-263","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct IMF press release with detailed program language and the Board approval announcement.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period fit.","ruler_attribution":"This is a formal policy commitment made under Sisi's presidency and his government; it does not itself prove personal honesty or separation from interests.","contrary_evidence":["AFTE and other monitors said the promised opening remained selective and that security practice continued to dominate."],"lenses":["7B.8","7B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Sisi approves State Ownership Policy Document","publisher":"State Information Service","publication_date":"2022-12-29","url":"https://sis.gov.eg/en/media-center/news/sisi-approves-state-ownership-policy-document/","claim":"The cabinet announced that President Abdel Fattah El Sisi approved the State Ownership Policy Document, which was meant to define the state's relation with the private sector and expand private-sector participation in development projects.","locator":"headline and lede","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official presidential/cabinet announcement of a named policy document and its stated purpose.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable as a presidential approval, but still only a formal policy commitment rather than evidence of implementation or ethical restraint.","contrary_evidence":["Later reporting and scholarship still described privileged state and military economic roles and limited private-sector opening."],"lenses":["7B.8","7B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Retain, Restructure, or Divest? Policy Options for Egypt's Military Economy","publisher":"Carnegie Endowment for International Peace","publication_date":"2022-01-31","url":"https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2022/01/retain-restructure-or-divest-policy-options-for-egypts-military-economy?lang=en","claim":"The paper argues that the military spearheaded Sisi's state-led investment strategy, controlled a vast economic portfolio, and benefited from privileged access and opacity that distorted private-sector development.","locator":"Introduction and cost-benefit analysis sections","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Well-cited policy paper from a major think tank that gives a direct, source-based analysis of Egypt's military economy.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Retrospective analysis of the Sisi-era economy that directly covers the 2022 ruler-period context.","ruler_attribution":"The paper directly ties the state-led strategy to Sisi's presidency, but the evidence is systemic and network-based rather than a forensic finding of personal enrichment.","contrary_evidence":["The same paper notes that some military projects did deliver public works, housing, and logistics benefits."],"lenses":["7B.3","7B.4","7B.5","7B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt's Sisi names Hassan Abdalla as caretaker cbank head","publisher":"Reuters via MarketScreener","publication_date":"2022-08-18","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/latest/Egypt-s-Sisi-names-Hassan-Abdalla-as-caretaker-cbank-head-41353858/","claim":"Sisi appointed Hassan Abdalla as caretaker central bank governor during the currency crisis; Abdalla came from state-owned media leadership and had long banking experience.","locator":"Reuters lede","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters-syndicated reporting gives a direct account of the presidential appointment and the appointee's background.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period fit.","ruler_attribution":"This is a direct presidential appointment and therefore attributable to Sisi; the appointee's banking background limits any simple nepotism inference.","contrary_evidence":["Abdalla was also described as a veteran banker with relevant experience, so the appointment is not proof of cronyism or incompetence."],"lenses":["7B.5","7B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Violations overshadow reform pledges.. The annual report on the state of freedom of expression in Egypt 2022","publisher":"Association of Freedom of Thought and Expression","publication_date":"2023-05-01","url":"https://afteegypt.org/en/research-en/2023/05/01/34214-afteegypt.html","claim":"AFTE said the government's 2022 reform language was empty rhetoric and that security and judicial practices continued to persecute opinion-makers, journalists, and creators; it documented 47 incidents with at least 63 violations against the press and media community in 2022.","locator":"lines 104-110 and 260-265","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"The report is advocacy-oriented but it gives specific case documentation, counts, and contemporaneous monitoring notes.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective 2023 report on 2022 conduct.","ruler_attribution":"The report attributes the pattern to Egyptian authorities under Sisi's presidency; it does not prove he personally ordered each case, but repeated notice and lack of correction support ruler attribution.","contrary_evidence":["The pardon committee did release some detainees, which AFTE treats as limited and selective corrective action."],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"No progress | The third quarterly report on the state of freedom of expression in Egypt (1 July - 30 September 2022)","publisher":"Association of Freedom of Thought and Expression","publication_date":"2022-11-15","url":"https://afteegypt.org/en/research-en/2022/11/15/32744-afteegypt.html","claim":"AFTE documented at least 59 violations in the third quarter of 2022, said the national dialogue had not become a real opening, and reported that the pardon committee released 520 political detainees and 13 convicts but with slow, selective results controlled in part by security bodies.","locator":"lines 111-128","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Quarterly monitoring report with named counts and process details; advocacy framing is present but the underlying documentation is specific.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period fit.","ruler_attribution":"The dialogue and pardon committee were created or reactivated by Sisi, but the report says security apparatuses still controlled key outcomes, which limits claims of genuine self-binding.","contrary_evidence":["The same report confirms that the president did call for dialogue and reactivate the pardon committee, so the record is mixed rather than purely negative."],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.8","7B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt: Arrests over calls for protests during COP27 expose reality of human rights crisis","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-11-06","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/11/egypt-arrests-over-calls-for-protests-during-cop27-expose-reality-of-human-rights-crisis/","claim":"Amnesty said Egyptian authorities arrested hundreds of people over protest calls before COP27, with at least 151 detainees under investigation and about 1,540 people investigated for free speech and association; it urged President al-Sisi to release those arbitrarily held.","locator":"lines 64-70 and 73-85","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Amnesty's press release is specific about the detention campaign, counts, and the timeline immediately before COP27.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period fit.","ruler_attribution":"The arrests were carried out by Egyptian authorities under Sisi's presidency in a highly visible national-security setting; direct presidential orders are not shown for each arrest, but the pattern is ruler-attributable through tolerance and notice.","contrary_evidence":["Authorities also used the pardon committee and released some prisoners, but Amnesty says that did not offset the wider detention campaign."],"lenses":["7B.7","7B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt: A Year of Whitewashing Despite Dire Crisis","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023-01-12","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/01/12/egypt-year-whitewashing-despite-dire-crisis","claim":"HRW said the Egyptian government failed to ease repression in 2022 or repeal laws curtailing basic freedoms, while civil society faced travel bans, asset freezes, and criminal investigations in retaliation for peaceful activism and criticism.","locator":"opening summary and repression paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"HRW's year-end news release directly summarizes its World Report findings and names the main mechanisms of repression.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective January 2023 summary of 2022 conduct.","ruler_attribution":"The pattern is tied to Sisi's government and the institutions he oversees; the release does not prove personal orders in every case, but it does show tolerated, repeated abuse under his rule.","contrary_evidence":["HRW notes the government's national dialogue and COP27 efforts, but treats them as mostly cosmetic whitewashing rather than real restraint."],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.8","7B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egyptian authorities arrest journalist Ahmed Fayez for reporting on Alaa Abdelfattah","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-11-14","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/11/egyptian-authorities-arrest-journalist-ahmed-fayez-for-reporting-on-alaa-abdelfattah/","claim":"CPJ said state security forces arrested journalist Ahmed Fayez after a Facebook post reporting that prison authorities were force-feeding Alaa Abdelfattah; Fayez was charged with false-news and terrorism-related offenses and held pending trial.","locator":"lines 516-521","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"CPJ identifies the arresting authority, the precipitating post, and the charges in a contemporaneous alert.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period fit.","ruler_attribution":"This is a direct state-security and prosecutorial action under Sisi's presidency; the report does not prove he personally ordered the arrest, but it is strong evidence of tolerated retaliation against truth-telling.","contrary_evidence":["The case arose from a journalist reporting on prison abuse rather than from any proven false allegation about private conduct."],"lenses":["7B.7","7B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egyptian authorities detain 3 journalists since September","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-11-09","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/11/egyptian-authorities-detain-3-journalists-since-september/","claim":"CPJ said Egyptian authorities had detained three journalists since September 2022 and warned that the arrests showed little concern for protecting journalists ahead of COP27.","locator":"search result lede / article lede","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"CPJ's contemporaneous alert names the journalists and the timing, and it is closely tied to the COP27 crackdown context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period fit.","ruler_attribution":"The arrests were made by Egyptian security forces during Sisi's presidency; the source supports a ruler-attributable pattern of intimidation rather than a single isolated case.","contrary_evidence":["The government publicly framed COP27 as a normal diplomatic event, but CPJ says the arrests undercut that claim."],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt releases 3,273 prisoners granted presidential pardons on occasion of Sinai Liberation","publisher":"Ahram Online","publication_date":"2022-04-27","url":"https://english.ahram.org.eg/News/465369.aspx","claim":"Egypt announced the release of 3,273 inmates under presidential pardon, and the move was presented as implementation of Sisi's decision to reactivate the Presidential Pardon Committee.","locator":"lines 57-76","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Ahram is state-affiliated and reliable for reporting the official decree and release count, though it is not independent on motive or broader evaluation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period fit.","ruler_attribution":"The pardon decision is directly attributable to Sisi, but the article also shows the action was procedural and selective rather than a broad transparency reform.","contrary_evidence":["AFTE later said the pardon process remained slow, selective, and security-controlled, so the release does not by itself establish sustained accountability."],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.6","7B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across IMF, World Bank, SIS/cabinet releases, Reuters syndications, Carnegie, AFTE, Amnesty, HRW, CPJ, and Ahram. I kept 12 distinct URLs because they provided the clearest ruler-specific integrity nexus. I rejected or deprioritized country-level corruption indices and macro reports that did not connect to Sisi's personal conduct, duplicate syndications of the same Reuters/AP story, and later background material that only repeated the 2022 pattern without adding a new integrity claim. I also excluded welfare, poverty, and business-climate pages that were useful context but did not by themselves show personal dishonesty, self-dealing, or obstruction. Where official reform pledges appeared, I kept them only when paired with contrary evidence showing the gap between pledge and practice.

**Remaining gaps**
The biggest gap is direct evidence of personal or family enrichment: I did not locate a 2022 asset declaration, audit trail, procurement file, or court finding tying Sisi or his close family to specific private gain from office. A second gap is direct truthfulness/correction evidence from speeches or written retractions, so 7B.1 and part of 7B.2 remain inferential rather than forensic. The strongest evidence is instead on tolerated repression, selective clemency, and public reform pledges that were not matched by durable openness. More search would help if you want a sharper distinction between systemic patronage, military capture, and direct self-enrichment.
